<Project>
  <PropertyGroup>
    <ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>true</ManagePackageVersionsCentrally>
  </PropertyGroup>
  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Avalonia UI (RemEx-jcma3) -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Avalonia" Version="12.1.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Avalonia.Desktop" Version="12.1.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Avalonia.Themes.Fluent" Version="12.1.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Avalonia.Fonts.Inter" Version="12.1.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Avalonia.Skia" Version="12.1.1" />

    <!-- NOT OPTIONAL, AND ITS ABSENCE IS SILENT. Up to Avalonia 11, UseSkia() implied HarfBuzz;
         in 12 it does not, and the package must be referenced and .UseHarfBuzz() called explicitly.
         Skip it and text still renders — Latin looks fine — while complex scripts lose shaping.
         RemEx ships Hindi (hi) among its nine locales, which is exactly the case that degrades, and
         nothing throws to say so. See Program.cs / App builder configuration. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Avalonia.HarfBuzz" Version="12.1.1" />

    <!-- REPLACES Avalonia.Diagnostics, which does not exist for Avalonia 12 — its NuGet listing
         stops at 11.3.20. DevTools moved to a separately versioned package, hence 2.2.3 rather than
         a 12.x number, and AttachDevTools() became AttachDeveloperTools(). -->
    <PackageVersion Include="AvaloniaUI.DiagnosticsSupport" Version="2.2.3" />

    <!-- Material.Avalonia (RemEx-851li) — the design system for the desktop overhaul, RemEx-kb4im.
         Referenced but INERT until RemEx-prkot includes the styles in App.axaml.

         THE CEILING THAT USED TO BE HERE IS GONE (RemEx-jcma3). This was pinned to 3.14.2 because
         3.15.0 onward require Avalonia 12.0.0 and the repo was on 11.3.11, which NuGet refused as
         a downgrade:
           error NU1605: Detected package downgrade: Avalonia from 12.0.0 to 11.3.11.
         That cap is what held the entire desktop Material epic. Avalonia is now on 12.1.1, so the
         constraint these packages declare is satisfied and they move to the current line.

         Worth keeping from the old note, because it is the fact that made the upgrade the answer
         rather than a workaround: the cap was enforced by NuGet, loudly, not by convention. A
         careless bump could never have slipped through quietly — it stopped the build. -->

    <PackageVersion Include="Material.Avalonia" Version="3.19.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Material.Avalonia.Dialogs" Version="3.19.0" />

    <!-- RESOLVED BY THE UPGRADE (RemEx-60x5f, closed by RemEx-jcma3). Kept as a record because the
         measurement below cost a day and the next person to reach for a transitive pin should read
         it. Material.Avalonia.Dialogs 3.19.0 depends on Avalonia.Controls.ItemsRepeater 12.0.0, so
         the stale resolution this describes cannot happen any more — the package's 11 line stopped
         at 11.1.5 and 12.0.0 is its next release, which is precisely why there was no 11.3.x to pin
         to and why the problem evaporates rather than being fixed.

         WHAT IT USED TO SAY, and what is still true about NuGet:

         A `PackageVersion` entry alone does NOT fix this — MEASURED. This was written as a pin to
         11.3.11 first, and NuGet ignored it in silence: restore and build both stayed green while
         artifacts/obj/remex.desktop/project.assets.json still resolved 11.0.9. Central package
         management binds versions only for packages a project actually references. A pin nobody
         checks is worse than no pin, because the comment above it reads as a guarantee.

         The documented fixes are CentralPackageTransitivePinningEnabled (a repo-wide behaviour
         change) or an explicit PackageReference to a package remex.desktop does not otherwise use.
         Stated from the NuGet docs, NOT verified here — an attempt to measure it did not produce a
         readable assets file and it was not worth a rabbit hole for a change we are not making.
         Whoever takes RemEx-60x5f should measure it rather than trust this paragraph.

         Left as-is deliberately. It builds clean and the package is inert until RemEx-prkot includes
         the Material styles.

         BE PRECISE ABOUT WHAT IS AND IS NOT GUARANTEED HERE (review). The >= 11.0.9 floor means
         restore resolves and the loader binds our higher 11.3.11 assemblies — so this will not fail
         to load. It does NOT mean a control compiled against 11.0.9 still BEHAVES correctly against
         11.3.11. Avalonia's 11.x compatibility has held for public API; control internals are where
         it has bent before. The residual risk is therefore behavioural and it lands at first use:
         a Material dialog that renders empty or lays out wrong, with no exception and nothing in the
         log. That is the silence class this repo keeps docs/REGRESSION-GUARDS.md for. Whoever wires
         Dialogs up should LOOK at the first Material dialog rather than trust the green build.
         RemEx-60x5f carries the decision. -->

    <!-- A separate package by design — Material.Avalonia does not bundle the icon pack. It was held
         at 3.0.0 for the same reason as the two above, one version later: 3.0.1 and up require
         Avalonia 12. On 12.1.1 that ceiling is gone, so this moves to the current stable 3.0.2.
         (3.0.3 exists only as nightlies; stable is what belongs in a pin.) RemEx-wyx2c consumes it. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Material.Icons.Avalonia" Version="3.0.2" />

    <!-- Microsoft Extensions & Standard Libraries -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration" Version="10.0.5" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration.Abstractions" Version="10.0.5" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection" Version="10.0.5" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Console" Version="10.0.5" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" Version="10.0.5" />
    <PackageVersion Include="System.Diagnostics.PerformanceCounter" Version="10.0.5" />
    <PackageVersion Include="System.Drawing.Common" Version="10.0.5" />
    <PackageVersion Include="System.Management" Version="10.0.5" />
    <PackageVersion Include="System.Threading.AccessControl" Version="10.0.5" />

    <!-- Other Libraries -->
    <PackageVersion Include="CommunityToolkit.Mvvm" Version="8.4.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="MaterialColorUtilities" Version="0.3.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="QRCoder" Version="1.6.0" />
    <!-- Also forced by Avalonia 12 (RemEx-jcma3): Avalonia.FreeDesktop 12.1.1 requires >= 0.94.1,
         so the old 0.21.3 fails restore. Consumed directly by the Linux portal input and
         remote-desktop session services. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Tmds.DBus.Protocol" Version="0.94.1" />

    <!-- Pinned Multicast DNS (Load-bearing, risk of drift/abandonment) -->
    <PackageVersion Include="Makaretu.Dns.Multicast" Version="0.27.0" />

    <!-- SkiaSharp 3.x, FORCED BY THE FRAMEWORK (RemEx-jcma3), not chosen.
         This used to read "2.88.x line - do not upgrade to 3.x, which has breaking changes", and
         that warning was correct for as long as the choice was ours. It is not any more:
         Avalonia.Skia 12.1.1 depends on SkiaSharp >= 3.119.4, so staying on 2.88.9 fails restore
         with NU1605 and there is no Avalonia 12 configuration that keeps the old line.
         The version is therefore the floor Avalonia declares, matched exactly. -->
    <PackageVersion Include="SkiaSharp" Version="3.119.4" />
    <PackageVersion Include="SkiaSharp.NativeAssets.Linux" Version="3.119.4" />

    <!-- Testing Packages -->
    <PackageVersion Include="coverlet.collector" Version="6.0.4" />
    <!-- Pinned FluentAssertions (7.0.0 line - v8 has license change) -->
    <PackageVersion Include="FluentAssertions" Version="7.0.0" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="17.14.1" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Moq" Version="4.20.72" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit" Version="2.9.3" />
    <PackageVersion Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="3.1.5" />
    <PackageVersion Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.Mvc.Testing" Version="10.0.5" />
  </ItemGroup>
</Project>
